-- title:
--   Theorem 2.1.3 — the disturbance-space formulation
-- statement:
--   A Markov Decision Model's stochastic transition law is often generated by an explicit
--   **disturbance** rather than specified directly as a kernel: a random variable $Z_{n+1}$, whose
--   law $Q_n^Z(\cdot \mid x, a)$ may depend on the current state and action, drives the next state
--   via a measurable **transition function** $T_n : D_n \times Z \to E$, so that
--   $X_{n+1} = T_n(X_n, a_n, Z_{n+1})$.
--
--   This theorem states that the two descriptions are equivalent: every transition kernel $Q_n$
--   arises from some disturbance representation, in the sense that
--
--   $$
--   Q_n(B \mid x, a) = Q_n^Z\bigl(\{z \in Z : T_n(x,a,z) \in B\} \bigm| x, a\bigr)
--   $$
--
--   for every measurable $B \subseteq E$; and conversely, every disturbance space $Z$, kernel
--   $Q_n^Z$, and measurable transition function $T_n$ define a genuine transition kernel $Q_n$ via
--   the same formula.
--
--   The forward direction is immediate (take $Z := E$, $T_n(x,a,z) := z$, and $Q_n^Z := Q_n$); the
--   converse direction shows that the displayed formula always produces a bona fide stochastic
--   kernel. The disturbance-space formulation is the standard way concrete examples (e.g. a wealth
--   process driven by an i.i.d. asset return) are set up as Markov Decision Models throughout the
--   book.
--
--   **Formalization Note.** The disturbance space $Z$ is quantified as a `Type` (rather than
--   `Type*`), matching the universe of the fixed state and action spaces $E, A$; both directions of
--   the "equivalently described" claim are stated as one existence statement each, following the
--   book's own two-paragraph proof.
-- source:
--   Bäuerle and Rieder, Markov Decision Processes with Applications to Finance, Universitext, Springer 2011, DOI 10.1007/978-3-642-18324-9, p. 15, Theorem 2.1.3

import Mathlib

open MeasureTheory ProbabilityTheory

namespace MDPFinance.Bellman

/-- Theorem 2.1.3 (Bäuerle–Rieder, p. 15, PDF 30): a Markov Decision Model is equivalently
described by data `(E, A, D_n, Z, T_n, Q_n^Z, r_n, g_N)`, where `Z` is a disturbance space,
`Q_n^Z(·|x,a)` a stochastic kernel giving the law of the disturbance `Z_{n+1}`, and
`T_n : D_n × Z → E` a measurable transition function with `X_{n+1} = T_n(X_n, a_n, Z_{n+1})`, in
the sense that `Q_n(B|x,a) = Q_n^Z(\{z : T_n(x,a,z) ∈ B\} | x,a)`. Both directions of the
equivalence are stated: every transition kernel `Q` arises from some disturbance representation
(the book's own proof takes `Z := E`, `T_n(x,a,z) := z`, `Q_n^Z := Q_n`), and every disturbance
representation induces a genuine transition kernel via the displayed formula. -/
theorem disturbance_space_equivalence {E A : Type*} [MeasurableSpace E] [MeasurableSpace A]
    (D : ℕ → Set (E × A)) (Q : ℕ → Kernel (E × A) E) :
    (∃ (Z : Type) (_ : MeasurableSpace Z) (QZ : ℕ → Kernel (E × A) Z) (T : ℕ → E × A → Z → E),
        (∀ n, Measurable (Function.uncurry (T n))) ∧
        ∀ n (xa : E × A) (B : Set E), MeasurableSet B →
          Q n xa B = QZ n xa {z | T n xa z ∈ B})
    ∧
    (∀ (Z : Type) [MeasurableSpace Z] (QZ : ℕ → Kernel (E × A) Z) (T : ℕ → E × A → Z → E),
        (∀ n, Measurable (Function.uncurry (T n))) →
        ∃ Q' : ℕ → Kernel (E × A) E,
          ∀ n (xa : E × A) (B : Set E), MeasurableSet B →
            Q' n xa B = QZ n xa {z | T n xa z ∈ B}) := by sorry

end MDPFinance.Bellman
